SECTION TITLE:
Complete Interactive Simulation Roadmap: Play Class to FSc

SECTION INTRO:
Gen Z Interactive Simulations is built to cover every subject of the Pakistani curriculum, from Play Group all the way to FSc (Intermediate, Class 11-12). The list below is the complete roadmap of interactive simulations that can be created for each level. Every topic includes a clear explanation of what the simulation should do, what the learner can control, and what the learner will understand by using it. Any teacher, student, or developer who opens this portal can use these descriptions as a blueprint to build, extend, or request a new simulation. Every simulation is designed to run directly in a web browser, with no installation, on mobile, tablet, and computer.

-----------------------------------------------------
LEVEL 1: PLAY GROUP, NURSERY, PREP (Ages 3-5)
-----------------------------------------------------
Goal: Build curiosity, recognition, and basic vocabulary through touch, sound, and colorful animation. Interfaces must use very large buttons, minimal text, friendly cartoon characters, and voice or sound feedback.

1. Shapes & Colors Sorter: The child drags circles, squares, triangles, rectangles, and stars into matching outlines. When a shape is placed correctly, it glows and its name and color are spoken in English and Urdu. This teaches shape recognition, color names, and hand-eye coordination.
2. Counting Fun 1-20: Animated objects such as apples, balloons, stars, and fish appear on the screen. The child taps each object, a number sound plays, and the total is shown at the end. A "How many?" mode asks the child to choose the correct number. This teaches one-to-one counting and number recognition.
3. Alphabet Adventure (A-Z and Urdu Haroof-e-Tahajji): Each letter is a large tile. Tapping it shows an animated picture (A for Apple, B for Ball, and Alif for Anar, Bay for Bakri) and plays the letter sound. A tracing mode lets the child draw the letter with a finger. This teaches letter shapes and phonics.
4. Animal Sounds & Homes: Farm animals and wild animals appear with their sounds. The child matches each animal to its home (barn, nest, den, pond, hive) and to its sound. This teaches animal names, habitats, and listening skills.
5. My Body Parts: A friendly cartoon child is shown. Touching eyes, nose, ears, mouth, hands, and feet highlights the part, says its name, and shows a simple function such as "Eyes help us see." This teaches body awareness and the five senses.
6. Day & Night and Weather Basics: A slider moves the sun and moon across the sky, changing the background from morning to night. Weather buttons switch between sunny, cloudy, rainy, and windy, with matching clothes the child can drag onto a character. This teaches time of day and weather vocabulary.
7. Fruits & Vegetables Basket: The child drags fruits and vegetables into the correct baskets and sees their color and name. A "healthy plate" mini game rewards healthy choices. This teaches food names, colors, and healthy eating.
8. Big & Small, Long & Short: Pairs of objects appear, and the child taps the bigger, smaller, longer, or shorter one. Objects can be stretched with a slider for comparison. This teaches early measurement language.
9. Pattern Maker: The child continues simple color or shape patterns (red-blue-red-blue). Correct answers complete a fun animation. This teaches early logic and sequencing.

-----------------------------------------------------
LEVEL 2: CLASS 1-2 (Ages 6-7)
-----------------------------------------------------
Goal: Introduce early reading, number operations, and basic science through guided play. Each lab has short instructions, picture hints, and instant "well done" feedback.

1. Addition & Subtraction Blocks: Colored blocks and an animated number line show how two numbers join (addition) or how some are taken away (subtraction). The child can change the numbers and watch the blocks and the hops on the number line update. This teaches the meaning of plus and minus with visual proof.
2. Place Value Abacus: A virtual abacus with ones, tens, and hundreds rods. The child slides beads and sees the number form, for example 2 hundreds + 4 tens + 7 ones = 247. This teaches place value and number building.
3. Clock Reading Lab: A large analog clock with draggable hour and minute hands and a digital clock that updates live. Challenge mode says "Set the time to half past 4." Includes AM/PM and day-routine pictures. This teaches reading and setting time.
4. Money & Shopping Simulator: A small shop with items priced in Pakistani rupees. The child picks Rs. 5, 10, 20, 50, 100 notes and coins to pay and receives change. This teaches money recognition, addition, and everyday math.
5. Skip Counting & Number Patterns: Frogs hop along a number line by 2s, 5s, and 10s, leaving colored marks. The child predicts the next numbers. This teaches the base for multiplication.
6. Shapes & Measurement Lab: The child measures objects with a virtual ruler in centimeters and compares lengths, weights (balance scale), and capacity (cups and jugs). This teaches basic measurement.
7. Living vs Non-Living Sorter: The child drags pictures (dog, stone, tree, car, bird, water) into "Living" or "Non-Living" and receives a short reason for each answer. This teaches the characteristics of living things.
8. Parts of a Plant & Seed Growth: Tapping root, stem, leaf, flower, and fruit shows the job of each part. A seed can be planted and given water and sunlight, and a time-lapse shows it growing. Too little water or light shows the plant wilting. This teaches what plants need.
9. Five Senses Lab: The child matches sense organs with activities (smelling a flower, tasting a lemon, hearing a bell, touching ice, seeing a rainbow). This teaches how we sense the world.
10. Good Habits & Safety: Interactive scenes teach hand washing, brushing teeth, road safety, and traffic light rules with simple choices and feedback.
11. Urdu & English Word Builder: Letters are dragged into slots to form simple words with a picture hint (cat, sun, bat, and Urdu equivalents). This teaches spelling and vocabulary.

-----------------------------------------------------
LEVEL 3: CLASS 3-5 (Primary)
-----------------------------------------------------
Goal: Build strong foundations in math and science with visual, hands-on experiments and simple quizzes.

MATHEMATICS
1. Multiplication Tables Visualizer: Arrays of dots and animated grids show 3 x 4 as three rows of four. The child chooses any table up to 20 and sees patterns. A speed quiz is included. This teaches multiplication as repeated addition.
2. Division & Sharing Lab: Items are shared equally among groups, with remainders shown clearly. This teaches division as fair sharing and connects it to multiplication.
3. Fractions Pizza & Bar Model: Pizzas and bars are sliced into equal parts. The child shades parts to build fractions, compares two fractions side by side, adds fractions with like denominators, and finds equivalent fractions. This teaches fractions visually.
4. Decimals & Percentages Lab: Sliders link fractions, decimals, and percentages on a single bar and on a 100-grid. This teaches that 1/2 = 0.5 = 50%.
5. Geometry Playground: The child builds shapes on a grid, measures sides and angles, and sees perimeter and area calculated live. This teaches 2D shapes, perimeter, and area.
6. Data Handling & Graphs: The class enters simple data (favorite fruit, favorite sport) and the lab instantly draws bar charts, pictographs, and pie charts. This teaches reading and creating graphs.
7. Time, Calendar & Money Word Problems: Interactive story problems with clocks, calendars, and shopping carts for practicing real-life math.

SCIENCE
8. States of Matter Simulator: A container of particles can be heated or cooled. The child watches particles vibrate, flow, or fly apart as the substance changes between solid, liquid, and gas, with a temperature meter. This teaches melting, freezing, boiling, and condensation.
9. Water Cycle Animation: The sun, sea, clouds, mountains, and rain are shown. The child changes temperature and sunlight to control evaporation, condensation, and rainfall. This teaches the continuous movement of water.
10. Food Chain & Food Web Builder: The child connects plants, insects, herbivores, carnivores, and decomposers with arrows. Removing one organism shows the effect on the others. This teaches energy flow and balance in nature.
11. Simple Machines Lab: Levers, pulleys, inclined planes, wheels and axles, and wedges can be tested. The child changes the load and effort and sees how the machine makes work easier. This teaches force and mechanical advantage in simple form.
12. Magnets Lab: The child drags magnets and objects, tests which materials are magnetic, and sees attraction and repulsion between poles. Iron filings show field lines. This teaches magnetism.
13. Light & Shadows: A torch and an object are placed on a table. The child moves them to see how shadow size, shape, and position change. Transparent, translucent, and opaque materials can be tested. This teaches how light travels in straight lines.
14. Solar System Explorer: Planets orbit the Sun at scaled speeds. Tapping a planet shows its size, distance, number of moons, and a fun fact. This teaches the order and features of planets.
15. Day, Night, Seasons & Earth's Tilt: Earth rotates and revolves around the Sun with a visible tilt. The child sees why day and night happen and why seasons change. This teaches Earth's motion.
16. Human Body Systems Intro: Tapping the skeleton, muscles, heart, lungs, and stomach shows what each does in simple animation.
17. Plants & Animals Classification: The child sorts organisms into mammals, birds, reptiles, fish, insects, and into flowering and non-flowering plants.

SOCIAL STUDIES AND GENERAL KNOWLEDGE
18. Map of Pakistan Explorer: Tap provinces, capitals, major cities, rivers, mountains (K2, Nanga Parbat), and deserts. A map-labeling quiz is included. This teaches Pakistan's geography.
19. Pakistan Landmarks & Culture: A tour of famous places (Badshahi Mosque, Mohenjo-daro, Minar-e-Pakistan, Faisal Mosque) with pictures and short facts.

