LEVEL 4: CLASS 6-8 (Middle School)
-----------------------------------------------------
Goal: Move from observation to explanation. Learners start using variables, graphs, and the scientific method.

GENERAL SCIENCE
1. Cell Structure Explorer (Plant vs Animal): A rotatable 3D cell. Tapping the nucleus, mitochondria, chloroplast, cell wall, vacuole, and other organelles highlights them and explains their function. A comparison toggle shows plant and animal cells side by side.
2. Microscope Simulator: The child adjusts focus, magnification (4x, 10x, 40x), and light to view onion peel, cheek cells, pond water, and leaf stomata. A drawing/labeling tool is included. This teaches microscope handling and cell observation.
3. Photosynthesis Lab: Sliders control light intensity, carbon dioxide, and water. The lab shows oxygen bubbles and glucose production and graphs the rate. This teaches the factors affecting photosynthesis.
4. Human Digestive System: Follow a piece of food from mouth to intestine. Enzymes, stomach acid, bile, and absorption are shown with timing. This teaches digestion step by step.
5. Respiratory & Circulatory Systems: Animated breathing with the diaphragm, gas exchange in alveoli, heartbeat, and blood flow through arteries and veins. A pulse rate experiment (rest vs exercise) is included.
6. Atoms, Elements & Compounds: The child builds atoms by adding protons, neutrons, and electrons and sees the element name, atomic number, and mass number change. Simple compounds like H2O and CO2 can be assembled. This teaches atomic structure basics.
7. Mixtures & Separation Techniques: Virtual lab benches for filtration, evaporation, distillation, sieving, magnetic separation, and paper chromatography. The child chooses the right method for each mixture.
8. Acids, Bases & Indicators: Test lemon juice, soap, vinegar, milk, and baking soda with litmus paper and universal indicator, and see the pH scale. This teaches properties of acids and bases.
9. Physical vs Chemical Changes: The child melts ice, burns paper, rusts iron, dissolves salt, and bakes dough, then classifies each change and sees the evidence (gas, color, heat, new substance).
10. Motion & Speed Lab: Cars move along a track. The child sets speed and time and sees a live distance-time graph. This teaches speed = distance / time and graph reading.
11. Force, Friction & Gravity: Boxes are pushed over different surfaces (ice, wood, sand) and on different planets. Force arrows and friction values are displayed. This teaches balanced and unbalanced forces.
12. Sound Waves Lab: Change frequency and amplitude to see wave shape and hear pitch and loudness. Compare sound in air, water, and vacuum. This teaches sound properties.
13. Light, Reflection & Mirrors: Rays reflect from plane mirrors with a measurable angle of incidence and reflection. A periscope builder is included.
14. Simple Electric Circuits: The child builds series and parallel circuits with batteries, bulbs, switches, and wires. A short circuit warning and conductor/insulator tests are included. This teaches how current flows.
15. Heat Transfer: Animated particles show conduction in a metal rod, convection in water, and radiation from the Sun. This teaches the three modes of heat transfer.
16. Weather, Climate & Rocks: Interactive weather station, rock cycle, and layers of the Earth.

MATHEMATICS
17. Algebra Balance Scale: Equations are shown as a balance. The child adds or removes weights on both sides to solve for x. This teaches the idea of equality in algebra.
18. Integers & Number Line: Positive and negative numbers are added, subtracted, and multiplied with a hopping character and a temperature or elevator analogy.
19. Ratio & Proportion Lab: Resize recipes, maps, and photos with sliders and see ratios stay equal. This teaches scale and unitary method.
20. Angles & Triangles Explorer: Drag triangle vertices to see all three angles and prove they always sum to 180 degrees. Classify triangles by sides and angles.
21. Area & Volume 3D: Rotate and unfold cubes, cuboids, cylinders, and prisms to understand surface area and volume formulas.
22. Percentages, Profit & Loss: A virtual shop with discounts and price tags to practice real-life calculations.
23. Statistics Basics: Mean, median, mode, and range are calculated and displayed on data entered by the learner.

COMPUTER SCIENCE
24. Block Coding (Scratch-style): Drag and snap blocks to move a character, draw shapes, and use loops and conditions. This teaches computational thinking.
25. Binary Number Converter: Flip bits on and off to see the decimal value and convert both ways. This teaches how computers store numbers.
26. Computer Hardware Explorer: Click on CPU, RAM, hard disk, and motherboard to see their roles, with an assembly challenge.

